---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import LeadForm from '../../components/LeadForm.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { PEST_GROUPS, pestsInGroup } from '../../data/pests';

const path = '/pest-library/';

/* The old site had a pest library hub but no profiles beneath it. The 48
   profiles are the topical-authority backbone — Phase 5. This hub exists now
   so each profile has a parent to link up to as it lands. */
const answer =
  'Our pest library covers the insects and rodents that actually turn up in Whatcom County homes and buildings — how to identify each one, when in the year it shows up here, what it does to a structure, and what treatment realistically involves.';

/* The species list lives in src/data/pests.ts and only there. It used to be a
   literal array here; once profiles got their own routes that array became a
   second copy of the enumeration and would have drifted from it — the defect
   this codebase keeps hitting. The hub now renders from the data file, links
   the profiles that exist, and shows the rest as plain names. */
const profiles = await getCollection('pests');
const written = new Set(profiles.filter((x) => x.data.ready === true).map((x) => x.data.species));

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I identify a pest I found in my house?',
    a: 'Take a clear photo next to something for scale and send it to us. Identification drives treatment — moisture ants and carpenter ants call for genuinely different responses, and getting the species wrong wastes a visit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are hobo spiders dangerous?',
    a: 'The Washington State Department of Health states that hobo spiders are not considered dangerous as previously thought, and that evidence does not support a link between their venom and skin necrosis. Most large fall spiders found indoors here are actually giant house spiders.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do we have termites in Whatcom County?',
    a: 'Yes, but not the aggressive subterranean species that drives treatment in the South. The Pacific dampwood termite is the common one here, and it goes after wood that is already wet — which usually means the real problem is a moisture source.',
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  path={path}
  title="Pest Library | Whatcom County, WA"
  description="Identification, seasonal behavior and treatment notes for the insects and rodents that actually show up in Whatcom County homes and buildings."
  kind="page"
  faqs={faqs}
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Pest library', path }]}
>
  <h1>Pest Library — What Actually Lives Here</h1>
  <AnswerBox text={answer} />

  {/* THE LEAD FORM SITS HERE, HIGH, and the position is the point.
      It used to hang off the foot of Cta.astro, which put it under three
      thousand words: a reader who had already decided to act had to scroll
      an entire article to find anything to act on. The quick answer still
      leads — it is one short paragraph and the schema's speakable selector
      points at it — and the form comes straight after.
  
      ONE PER PAGE. The GHL loader binds to its iframe by id, so a second
      copy would break both; the CTA at the foot no longer carries one and
      keeps its phone button. The sticky mobile bar jumps to this. */}
  <LeadForm />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Most pest libraries are national copy pasted onto a local site, which is
      why they list species that have never been recorded in this county. This
      one covers what our technicians actually find in Whatcom County, with the
      seasonal timing that applies here rather than to a national average.
    </p>

    <h2>The four things worth knowing about pests in Whatcom County</h2>

    <h3>Damp matters more than cold</h3>
    <p>
      We do not get a hard winter, so populations are never reset. What we get
      instead is about thirty-five inches of rain spread across a long gray
      season, which keeps wood damp year-round. That single fact explains most of
      our call volume. Dampwood termites only attack wood that is already wet.
      Moisture ants nest in it. Carpenter ants prefer wood that has begun to
      soften. Silverfish, sowbugs and millipedes are all humidity-driven. When one
      of those appears indoors here, the useful question is where the water is,
      not which product to spray.
    </p>

    <h3>Most of what frightens people here is harmless</h3>
    <p>
      The giant house spider is the largest spider most people in this county will
      ever see indoors, and it is not dangerous. Its close relative the hobo
      spider carries a reputation built on research that has since been walked
      back — the Washington State Department of Health states plainly that hobo
      spiders are not considered dangerous as previously thought, and that
      evidence does not support the claimed link to skin necrosis. We include this
      because scaring customers is an easy way to sell treatments and we would
      rather not.
    </p>

    <h3>Our termites are not the ones you have read about</h3>
    <p>
      Termite content online is overwhelmingly written about aggressive
      subterranean species in the South and East. The common species here is the
      Pacific dampwood termite, which is a moisture-follower rather than a
      structural predator of sound wood. Finding them is genuinely bad news, but
      the news is usually about a water problem that has been running for a while
      rather than about an invading colony.
    </p>

    <h3>What this library does not cover</h3>
    <p>
      Birds and nuisance wildlife are not in here, because they are not what we
      do. Sasquatch is a pest control and exclusion company, and bird work,
      bats, squirrels, raccoons and opossums are all somebody else's specialty.
      Several of those are federally or state protected with hard seasonal
      timing attached, and that is work for an operator set up to handle it
      properly rather than for us.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that is what you have, call anyway — we will tell you what it sounds
      like from the description and point you somewhere useful.
    </p>

    <h3>Identification is most of the value</h3>
    <p>
      A surprising share of what we get called about needs no treatment at all,
      and the difference between the two is nearly always identification. A
      carpenter ant and a moisture ant call for genuinely different responses. A
      giant house spider and a hobo spider are routinely confused, and only one
      of those confusions leads to unnecessary treatment. Winged carpenter ants
      in spring get mistaken for termite swarmers constantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why these entries lead with what the thing actually is and what it
      is telling you about the building, rather than with a treatment.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use this library</h2>
    <p>
      Each profile covers identification, when in the year it shows up in this
      county, what it actually does to a structure or to people, the signs that
      distinguish it from the species it gets confused with, and what treatment
      realistically involves. Where the honest answer is that no treatment is
      warranted, the profile says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have something in front of you and cannot place it, photograph it
      next to a coin or a fingertip for scale and send it over. Identification is
      free and it is the step that determines everything after it.
    </p>
    <h2>When to call and when not to</h2>
    <p>
      Not everything in this library warrants a treatment, and we would rather say
      so here than after you have paid for a visit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Usually does not need us:</strong> a single large spider indoors in
      autumn, a few ants scouting a counter in early spring, one wasp in a room
      with an open window, a cluster of conifer seed bugs on a sunny wall in
      October. These are seasonal and self-limiting.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Worth a call:</strong> droppings anywhere in a kitchen or pantry,
      scratching in a wall or ceiling at night, sawdust-like frass below trim,
      soft or spongy wood around a window or sill, an ant trail that persists
      after cleaning, any wasp or hornet nest near a door or a play area, or bites appearing overnight.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Worth a call quickly:</strong> anything structural you want sealed
      before autumn, because the useful window for exclusion work is August and
      the queue for it fills up.
    </p>

  </div>
    <h2>A note on sources</h2>
    <p>
      Where a profile makes a claim about risk, law or biology, it says where that
      comes from — Washington State Department of Health for the spider guidance,
      and WSU Extension for regional species behavior. We date those references so
      you can see how current they are.
    </p>
    <p>
      That matters more in this field than it sounds. Pest content online is
      dominated by material written to sell rather than to inform, and a good deal
      of what circulates about hobo spiders, "murder hornets" and termites in the
      Pacific Northwest is either outdated or was never accurate here.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not in this library</h2>
    <p>
      You will not find fire ants, Formosan termites or brown recluse profiles
      here, because none of them is established in Whatcom County. They appear on
      a great many Pacific Northwest pest sites anyway, copied from national
      templates, and every time they do they make a local site slightly less
      trustworthy.
    </p>
    <p>
      The black widow is a separate case and we would rather draw the line
      accurately than tidily. It is a genuine Washington spider rather than a
      national template entry: the state Department of Health describes the
      western black widow as common to eastern Washington, and its range follows
      the drier, warmer country east and south of the Cascades. On this side of
      the mountains it is uncommon, and the ones that do turn up are in woodpiles,
      meter boxes and undisturbed shed corners rather than in living space. That
      is a different statement from saying the species does not occur in the
      state, and we are not going to make the stronger one just because it reads
      more cleanly next to the recluse.
    </p>
    <p>
      The widow is also only half of what the state actually says, and it is the
      half this page had been giving all the room to. The Department of Health
      names two spiders in Washington as being of medical significance, and the
      second is the yellow sac spider — which, unlike the widow, is common on
      this side of the Cascades. It is small, pale and easy to miss: it spends
      the day in a silk pocket where a wall meets a ceiling and hunts across
      walls, floors and bedding at night rather than waiting in a web, which is
      exactly why it is the one realistically likely to bite somebody in a
      Whatcom County bedroom. The bite itself is a minor event — a sharp sting,
      then redness and itching that settle within a day or two — and the
      skin-necrosis stories attached to it are as poorly supported as the ones
      attached to the hobo spider. Both halves of that are worth having, which is
      why it gets <a href="/pest-library/yellow-sac-spider/">a profile of its
      own</a> rather than a line in somebody else's.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have been told you have a brown recluse here, the identification is
      worth a second look before anyone treats for it.
    </p>


  {PEST_GROUPS.map((g) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{g.name}</h2>
      <p class="blurb">{g.blurb}</p>
      <ul class="rail">
        {pestsInGroup(g.key).map((s) =>
          written.has(s.slug)
            ? <li><a href={`/pest-library/${s.slug}/`}>{s.name}</a></li>
            : <li><span>{s.name}</span></li>,
        )}
      </ul>
    </section>
  ))}

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <Cta market="Whatcom County" />
</BaseLayout>

{/* No scoped <style> here, on purpose. The group blurbs and rail counts used to be styled locally,
    out of hard-coded hex fallbacks and opacity — including var(--tint),
    a token that never existed, so every one of those rules silently
    resolved to its fallback and could not follow a brand change. It is
    styled from the token system in src/styles/global.css instead. */}
